<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thomas Paine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1737">1737</a>–<a href="/years/1809">1809</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Thomas Pain</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thomas Paine was an English-born American Founding Father, political philosopher and statesman whose pamphlets Common Sense (1776) and The American Crisis (1776–1783) helped inspire the colonists to declare independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thetford">Thetford</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norfolk">Norfolk</a>, he came to the British American colonies in 1774 with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-franklin">Benjamin Franklin</a>. His Rights of Man (1791), a defence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-burke">Edmund Burke</a>, led to his conviction in absentia for seditious libel in 1792. Elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">French National Convention</a>, he was held in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> Prison from December 1793 to November 1794, and in The Age of Reason he advocated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deism">Deism</a> and argued against Christian doctrine.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thomas-paine.jpg" alt="Thomas Paine" width="330" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Laurent Dabos · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laurent_Dabos_–_Thomas_Paine_–_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Thomas Paine was the son of Joseph Pain, a tenant farmer and stay-maker, and Frances Pain, born Cocke. His father was a Quaker and his mother an Anglican. He attended Thetford Grammar School from 1744 to 1749 and at 13 was apprenticed to his father. At 19 he served as a privateer aboard the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, and in 1759 he became a master staymaker in Sandwich, Kent. He married Mary Lambert that year, but his business collapsed, and she and their child died in early labour at Margate. He was using the spelling Paine by 1769, while living in Lewes, Sussex.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Paine barely survived the voyage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>, where typhoid fever killed five passengers, and in March 1775 he became editor of the Pennsylvania Magazine. Common Sense appeared on 10 January 1776, signed by an Englishman. He estimated that it sold 100,000 copies in three months, though some historians dispute such figures. Benjamin Rush suggested the title, since Paine had called it Plain Truth.</p><p>Rights of Man, a tract of some 90,000 words answering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-burke">Edmund Burke</a>, appeared on 13 March 1791. Its second part proposed state support for the poor, public education and pensions for workers from the age of 50. Agrarian Justice argued that landed property had robbed most people of a natural inheritance, and proposed a national fund paying fifteen pounds to each person at the age of twenty-one. In The Age of Reason he wrote that he believed in one God and in no church. He was also an abolitionist, and Rush later credited him with a Pennsylvania Magazine article against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Paine stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> until 1802 and returned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a>'s invitation, to find himself disliked by the devout and by the Federalists. He died on 8 June 1809 at 59 Grove Street in Greenwich Village, aged 72. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Quakers</a> refused to bury him in their graveyard, so he was buried on his farm at New Rochelle. In 1819 William Cobbett dug up the bones to give him a reburial in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> that never took place, and they were later lost. His writings gave radical societies in England and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> in the early 1790s a boost to their confidence. A marble column raised in New Rochelle in 1839 is the oldest memorial to him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thomas Paine” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>(1789) the Abbé Sieyès attacked the French nobility as a privileged caste living off the nation, and in Rights of Man (1791) <b>Thomas Paine</b> called the idea of hereditary legislators “as absurd as an hereditary mathematician”.</blockquote><a href="/government/aristocracy">Aristocracy · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Thomas Paine</b>’s Common Sense (1776) denounced hereditary succession as an imposition on posterity and declared one honest man worth more to society “than all the crowned ruffians that ever lived”; in Rights of Man (1791) he argued that the idea of hereditary legislators was as absurd as that…</blockquote><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>Karl Marx rejected the foundations of liberal theory, and Vladimir Lenin held that liberal science defends wage slavery, although liberals such as <b>Thomas Paine</b> also criticised wage slavery.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/liberalism">Liberalism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>The first pamphlet of <b>Thomas Paine</b>'s series The American Crisis appeared in The Pennsylvania Journal, signed &quot;Common Sense&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-19">19 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thetford">Thetford</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Greenwich Village, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.41,0.74&amp;year=1783" aria-label="Thetford on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4567 355 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Thomas Paine"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5017 625h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5017 625h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.41,0.74&amp;year=1783">What was here in 1783? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Government, like dress, is the badge of lost innocence; the palaces of kings are built on the ruins of the bowers of paradise.&#8221;</p><footer>Common Sense (1776) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/147/pg147-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Tyranny, like hell, is not easily conquered; yet we have this consolation with us, that the harder the conflict, the more glorious the triumph. What we obtain too cheap, we esteem too lightly: it is dearness only that gives every thing its value.&#8221;</p><footer>The American Crisis, no. 1 (December 1776) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3741/pg3741-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Independence is my happiness, and I view things as they are, without regard to place or person; my country is the world, and my religion is to do good.&#8221;</p><footer>Rights of Man, part 2, chapter 5 (1792) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3742/pg3742-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The summer soldier and the sunshine patriot will, in this crisis, shrink from the service of their country; but he that stands it now, deserves the love and thanks of man and woman.&#8221;</p><footer>The American Crisis, no. 1 (December 1776) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3741/pg3741-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;We have it in our power to begin the world over again. A situation, similar to the present, hath not happened since the days of Noah until now.&#8221;</p><footer>Common Sense (1776) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/147/pg147-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Society in every state is a blessing, but government even in its best state is but a necessary evil; in its worst state an intolerable one…&#8221;</p><footer>Common Sense (1776) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/147/pg147-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
